			"Date": "2026-05-25T08:35:21Z",
			"File": [
				"⠇⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				26,
				2
			],
			"Message": "feat(Land): Advance Element submodules — hierarchy prepare routing and config notifications\n\nAdvance Cocoon and Mountain submodules to fix language provider method dispatch and extend IPC notification coverage for the extension host.\n\nCocoon extends its GRPCServerService method router to match `$prepareCallHierarchyItems`, `$prepareTypeHierarchyItems`, and similar `$prepare*` language feature invocations alongside the existing `$provide*` and `$resolve*` patterns. Previously, the regex gate would drop prepare-method requests before they reached InvokeLanguageProvider, causing call and type hierarchy features to silently fail in the extension host.\n\nMountain forwards configuration update events to Cocoon through a fire-and-forget Vine notification, enabling `vscode.workspace.onDidChangeConfiguration` to fire for extensions like rust-analyzer, ESLint, and Prettier that react to workspace config changes. The changed key is included in the payload so Cocoon can scope the change event; a Vine send failure must not propagate back and fail the caller's awaiting IPC response.\n\nBoth hierarchy intelligence and config-change reactivity now function correctly across the Mountain ↔ Cocoon gRPC boundary.",
			"SHA": "4dd20438695e32f57af2b5d894c2c03a18bb085c"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-25T08:29:53Z",
			"File": [
				"⡟⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				26,
				4
			],
			"Message": "feat(Land): Advance Element submodules — terminal shell integration chain plus eliminate tooling fixes\n\nAdvance Cocoon, Sky, Mountain, and Maintain submodules to their latest revisions.\n\nCocoon implements `$acceptTerminalShellIntegrationActivated` and `$acceptTerminalCwdChange` notification handlers from Sky Bridge, setting a minimal `shellIntegration` stub on matching terminals and parsing OSC 633 `P;cwd=` sequences into URI-like objects so extensions can read `shellIntegration.cwd.fsPath`.\n\nMountain exposes the backend half of the same feature: `setShellIntegrationActive` and `setCwd` gRPC notifications that persist the shell's working directory in ApplicationState. It also adds process introspection IPC handlers (getPlatform, getArch, getPid, getExecPath, getMemoryInfo, getCpuInfo) for Wind's shared-process diagnostics, differentiates ENOENT as NotFound across FileRead/Readdir/Stat, and fixes the language configuration Tauri event emission to forward the full params object (language + rules) instead of only the language string.\n\nSky parses VS Code shell integration OSC 633 sequences from terminal output in its bridge layer, forwarding them to Mountain via MountainIPCInvoke so the Cocoon → Mountain → Sky chain completes for `terminal.shellIntegration.cwd` and activation detection.\n\nMaintain accumulates multiple fixes and merges across the eliminate tool: span-based text patching that preserves inline comments, blank lines, and original indentation (replacing the prettyplease full-reprint path); a new --reformat CLI flag to opt into the old behaviour; loop-body inlining safety that prevents initialiser expressions from being moved inside for/while/loop bodies (changing evaluation semantics); and free-variable-move-safety checks that skip inlining when a binding's Init references variables moved before the substitution site.",
			"SHA": "42c424dbf2278960aa68a23d0080a52db1d8318f"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-25T08:29:21Z",
			"File": [
				"⠃⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				26,
				1
			],
			"Message": "fix(Maintain): Skip inlining when free vars in Init are moved before substitution site\n\nAdvance the Maintain submodule to include the fix for premature inlining of bindings whose Init expression references free variables that are later moved before the substitution site. This E0382 regression occurred when a binding like `let display = var.clone()` passed all safety checks (count=1, not in closure, not oversized) but was inlined after `var` was moved into a struct field, placing the clone after the move.\n\nThe new `IsFreeVarSafe` function in Safe.rs collects plain-identifier free variables from Init, then scans intervening statements for by-value uses (function arguments, struct field shorthands, assignments) of those variables. Candidates with moved free vars are skipped. The check is conservative — it only inspects Expr::Path single-segment identifiers — guaranteeing no false negatives that produce compile errors, at the cost of possible false positives that keep suboptimal bindings live.\n\nA regression test in Inline.rs covers the exact get_file_info / get_configuration pattern from Mountain's AirClient sidecar.",
			"SHA": "05b68391922170f5b7b40b214d82456466f38f6b"
		},
